---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import ComponentRows from '../../components/ComponentRows.astro'
import Explainer from '../../components/Explainer.astro'
import { snippets } from '../../data/components.js'
import { diagrams } from '../../data/diagrams.js'
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/paths.js'

const repo = 'https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut/blob/main'

const clone = `git clone https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut.git
cd suitecut`

const buildSdk = `cmake --preset release -S native/client
cmake --build .suitecut/build/client-release --parallel 2
ctest --test-dir .suitecut/build/client-release --output-on-failure -L unit -j 1
cmake --install .suitecut/build/client-release --prefix "$PWD/.suitecut/sdk-install"`

const runtime = `./bin/suitecut --session demo open https://example.com
./bin/suitecut --session demo snapshot
./bin/suitecut --session demo close

./bin/suitecut-native-record --url https://example.com \\
  --output /tmp/example.webm --duration-ms 5000 --fps 29.97 --audio`
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Build suitecut's C++ SDK and CEF engine, then drive a page from C++, C, the command line or MCP."
  section="overview"
>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p>
    suitecut is a browser engine built on CEF, and the native code around it. There is no Node.js
    package: you link the C++ SDK, call its C ABI, or run the <code>suitecut-native-control</code>
    command line, which is also an MCP server.
  </p>
  <ComponentRows />
  <p>
    To see what happens between a paint and a finished recording, read
    <a href={sitePath('/docs/how-it-works')}>how it works</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <p>
    The C++ SDK needs CMake 3.20 or newer, a C++20 compiler, and the threads, OpenSSL and ZLIB
    development libraries. It needs no CEF headers, and it can drive an installed Chrome, Chromium
    or Firefox without the engine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The engine needs CMake 3.21 or newer, Ninja, the exact CEF SDK named in
    <code>native/browser/cef-build.json</code> and a pinned libyuv source. It is pinned to CEF 156.0.30
    on stable Chromium 156.0.8078.96. Recording and streaming need FFmpeg and FFprobe.
  </p>
  <p>
    Linux x64 and macOS ARM64 are qualified separately. macOS is qualified on 27.0.1 with Apple
    silicon; older releases and Intel Macs aren't. Windows runtime packaging isn't implemented. The <a
      href={`${repo}/docs/building.md`}>build requirements</a
    > list every dependency.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build-the-sdk">Build the SDK</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="Terminal" lang="bash" code={clone} />
  <CodeBlock label="Terminal" lang="bash" code={buildSdk} />
  <p>
    Consumers find the installed package with CMake and link one target. Use
    <code>-DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON</code> in a separate build directory for a shared library.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="CMake" lang="plaintext" code={snippets.clientStart} />

  <h2 id="build-the-engine">Build the engine</h2>
  <p>
    Follow the <a href={`${repo}/native/browser/README.md`}>engine build instructions</a>, which
    fetch the pinned CEF SDK and libyuv. On macOS the engine is the executable inside
    <code>suitecut-browser.app</code>; keep the helper apps and the CEF framework next to it. Build
    with one job on a small Mac.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Terminal" lang="bash" code={snippets.engineStart} />
  <p>
    On Linux x64, the <a href={`${repo}/docs/native-full-runtime.md`}>full runtime archive</a> holds the
    SDK, the C ABI, the command line, the recorder, the engine, CEF and FFmpeg in one relocatable directory:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Terminal" lang="bash" code={runtime} />

  <h2 id="drive-a-page">Drive a page</h2>
  <p>
    The C++ facade has browsers, contexts, pages and locators. The same calls launch the engine, an
    installed Chrome with <code>Browser::launch_chromium</code>, or Firefox with
    <code>Browser::launch_firefox</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="C++" lang="cpp" code={snippets.cppCode} />
  <p>
    The <a href={sitePath('/examples')}>examples</a> are complete programs from
    <code>native/client/examples</code>, including the C ABI and an agent loop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="record-a-page">Record a page</h2>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.recording} />
  <p>
    The engine records WebM with page audio, or hardware H.264 MP4 on macOS. Commands and options
    are on <a href={sitePath('/docs/recording')}>recording and streaming</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
